  view_file {
    absolute_path_uri: "file:///skills/system_skills/threejs/SKILL.md"
    end_line: 334
    content: "---\nname: threejs\ndescription: >-\n  Comprehensive guide for Three.js development. Use when creating 3D scenes,\n  animating objects, handling user interaction, loading assets, applying\n  materials and textures, or creating custom shaders and post-processing\n  effects.\n---\n\n# Three.js Skill\n\nThis skill provides a comprehensive reference for Three.js development, split\ninto specialized topics.\n\n> [!IMPORTANT] **MANDATORY REFERENCE LOADING**\n>\n> Before writing any Three.js or WebGPU/TSL code, you **MUST** identify the\n> relevant topic(s) below and read the corresponding reference file(s) (e.g.\n> `/skills/system_skills/threejs/references/fundamentals.md`). Do NOT rely on\n> generic assumptions without reading the topic references first.\n\n## Topics\n\n-   **[Fundamentals](references/fundamentals.md)**: Scene setup, cameras,\n    renderer, Object3D hierarchy, coordinate systems.\n-   **[Animation](references/animation.md)**: Keyframe animation, skeletal\n    animation, morph targets, animation mixing.\n-   **[Geometry](references/geometry.md)**: Built-in shapes, BufferGeometry,\n    custom geometry, instancing.\n-   **[Interaction](references/interaction.md)**: Raycasting, controls,\n    mouse/touch input, object selection.\n-   **[Lighting](references/lighting.md)**: Light types, shadows, environment\n    lighting.\n-   **[Loaders](references/loaders.md)**: Asset loading - GLTF, textures,\n    images, models, async patterns.\n-   **[Materials](references/materials.md)**: PBR, basic, phong, shader\n    materials, material properties.\n-   **[Post-Processing](references/postprocessing.md)**: EffectComposer, bloom,\n    DOF, screen effects.\n-   **[Shaders](references/shaders.md)**: GLSL, ShaderMaterial, uniforms, custom\n    effects.\n-   **[Textures](references/textures.md)**: Texture types, UV mapping,\n    environment maps, texture settings.\n\n## WebGPU & TSL Topics\n\n-   **[WebGPU Reference](references/webgpu-reference.md)**: TSL syntax, types,\n    operators, and quick reference.\n-   **[WebGPU Core Concepts](references/webgpu-core-concepts.md)**: Variables,\n    control flow, functions, and time.\n-   **[WebGPU Materials](references/webgpu-materials.md)**: Node materials, PBR\n    properties, and vertex manipulation.\n-   **[WebGPU Compute Shaders](references/webgpu-compute-shaders.md)**: Storage\n    buffers, workgroups, and atomic operations.\n-   **[WebGPU Post-Processing](references/webgpu-post-processing.md)**:\n    RenderPipeline, built-in effects, and custom effects.\n-   **[WGSL Integration](references/webgpu-wgsl-integration.md)**: Embedding raw\n    WGSL code in TSL.\n-   **[WebGPU Device Loss](references/webgpu-device-loss.md)**: Handling GPU\n    device loss and recovery.\n-   **[WebGPU Limits & Features](references/webgpu-limits-and-features.md)**:\n    Querying and requesting GPU limits.\n-   **[WebGPU Examples](references/webgpu-examples.md)**: Complete examples of\n    WebGPU scenes.\n-   **[WebGPU Templates](references/webgpu-templates.md)**: Starter templates\n    for projects and compute shaders.\n\n## General Best Practices\n\n1.  **Resource Management**: Always dispose of geometries, materials, and\n    textures when they are no longer needed to prevent memory leaks.\n2.  **Performance**: Limit draw calls by merging geometries or using\n    `InstancedMesh`. Use appropriate texture sizes and segment counts.\n3.  **Color Space**: Ensure correct color space settings (usually\n    `SRGBColorSpace` for color textures and output).\n4.  **3D Game Development (`game-dev` Skill Integration)**: When building\n    interactive 3D games (with player controls, physics, collision detection,\n    scoring, health, and game state transitions), check if the **`game-dev`**\n    skill (`game_dev/SKILL.md`) is present in your available skills list. **If\n    available**, read `game_dev/SKILL.md` to combine game loop patterns,\n    responsive keyboard/mouse/touch controls, and game state management with the\n    Three.js studio PBR rendering and zero-404 asset directives below. If the\n    `game-dev` skill is not available, proceed directly using the Three.js\n    directives below with a `THREE.Clock` (`getDelta()`) game loop.\n\n## Three.js Architecture, Performance, Visual Realism & UX Directives\n\n> **Purpose**: These directives act as an additive quality, performance,\n> rendering, and UX enhancer on top of your natural domain reasoning and modular\n> application architecture.\n\n### 1. Respect Your Modular Architecture & Creative Depth\n\n-   **Preserve Modular Decomposition**: Whether you are building a multi-file\n    TypeScript/React application (`/src/...`) or a single-file HTML experience,\n    retain your full, deep domain decomposition (e.g., dedicated modules for\n    procedural geometry generation, material libraries, kinematics/physics,\n    lighting rigs, audio synthesis, and UI state).\n-   **Never Compromise Domain Richness**: Do not simplify or omit intricate\n    mechanical, mathematical, or visual details. Proceed with your full\n    hierarchical blueprint, realistic sub-components, and coordinated\n    mechanical/physical ratios.\n-   **Composite 3\342\200\22312 Part Procedural Models (`THREE.Group`)**: Never represent a\n    hero subject, vehicle, character, tree, or structure with a single default\n    primitive (`BoxGeometry`, `SphereGeometry`, `CylinderGeometry`,\n    `CapsuleGeometry`). Assemble procedural 3D subjects as a `THREE.Group` of\n    **3\342\200\22312 distinct parts** across three silhouette scales: **(1) Primary\n    silhouette** (hull, trunk, main body), **(2) Secondary read** (wheels,\n    canopy, turret, wings, roof), and **(3) Accent detail** (visor, headlights,\n    exhaust, trim), using `ExtrudeGeometry` (with bevels), `LatheGeometry`, or\n    `TubeGeometry` where appropriate. Vary `roughness` by `>= 0.25` (and\n    `metalness`) between adjacent parts, apply subtle per-instance scale\n    (`\302\26115%`) and `setColorAt` tint variation on `InstancedMesh` foliage or\n    rocks, and pivot every prop and actor at bottom-center so it sits flush on\n    the ground (`groundHeightAt(x, z)`) with nothing floating.\n-   **Natural Outdoor Perimeters vs. Indoor Walls**: Indoor scenes use walls,\n    but **outdoor arenas, villages, forests, and terrains must never be boxed\n    inside a rectangular indoor wall floating in an empty void**\342\200\224extend terrain\n    beyond the playable zone and bound outdoor perimeters with natural barriers\n    (cliffs, rock outcrops, dense tree groves, water/coast, fences) and horizon\n    fog fade-off.\n-   **Zero External File / 404 Dependencies**: All textures, surface finishes,\n    normal maps, gear teeth profiles, and audio must be procedurally generated\n    in code (HTML5 2D Canvas, `DataTexture`, mathematical `ExtrudeGeometry` /\n    `LatheGeometry` / `BufferGeometry`, and Web Audio API oscillators). Never\n    reference unhosted local `.png`, `.glb`, `.json`, or `.mp3` files.\n\n### 2. Rendering, PBR Materials & Texture Fidelity\n\nWhen configuring your Three.js renderer, materials (`materials.ts`), and\nprocedural textures, enforce these exact physical rendering standards:\n\n1.  **Mandatory Studio Environment Reflection (`RoomEnvironment`)**:\n\n    -   High-metalness materials (`metalness > 0.5`: brushed gold, platinum,\n        blued steel, chrome) and transmissive glass (`transmission > 0.8`:\n        sapphire crystal, optical lenses) appear dark or flat without an\n        environment map.\n    -   Always generate a zero-dependency procedural studio environment map\n        during scene initialization:\n\n        ```ts\n        import { RoomEnvironment } from\n          \'three/addons/environments/RoomEnvironment.js\';\n\n        const pmremGenerator = new THREE.PMREMGenerator(renderer);\n        scene.environment = pmremGenerator.fromScene(\n          new RoomEnvironment(),\n          0.04,\n        ).texture;\n        pmremGenerator.dispose();\n        ```\n\n2.  **Modern Renderer & Color Pipeline (Three.js r160+)**:\n\n    ```ts\n    const isHeadless =\n      navigator.webdriver || /HeadlessChrome/i.test(navigator.userAgent);\n    renderer.outputColorSpace = THREE.SRGBColorSpace;\n    renderer.toneMapping = THREE.ACESFilmicToneMapping;\n    renderer.toneMappingExposure = 1.1;\n    // Cap at 1.0 in headless CPU WebGL (playwright-cli sets\n    // navigator.webdriver=false), or 2.0 on desktop/mobile GPUs.\n    renderer.setPixelRatio(\n      isHeadless ? 1 : Math.min(window.devicePixelRatio, 2),\n    );\n    renderer.shadowMap.enabled = !isHeadless;\n    renderer.shadowMap.type = THREE.PCFSoftShadowMap;\n    ```\n\n3.  **Advanced PBR Material & Shader Extension (`MeshPhysicalMaterial` &\n    `onBeforeCompile`)**:\n\n    -   **Anisotropic Brushed Metals**: For luxury brushed gold, titanium, or\n        steel, enable `anisotropy: 0.8` and `anisotropyRotation` alongside\n        `metalness: 0.95` and `roughness: 0.15`.\n    -   **Optical Glass & Crystals**: Use `transmission: 0.95`, `ior: 1.55 -\n        2.4`, `thickness: 1.5`, `attenuationColor`, and `clearcoat: 1.0`.\n    -   **Extending Built-in PBR Materials (`onBeforeCompile`)**: When adding\n        procedural waves, ripples, or vertex displacement to an object that\n        still needs full PBR lighting, shadows, and environment reflections,\n        extend `MeshStandardMaterial` / `MeshPhysicalMaterial` via\n        `material.onBeforeCompile` (injecting custom GLSL at `#include\n        <begin_vertex>` or `#include <color_fragment>`) rather than falling back\n        to unlit `ShaderMaterial`.\n\n4.  **Procedural Texture Color Space & Anisotropy**:\n\n    -   **Color Space**: For procedurally generated color/albedo maps\n        (`CanvasTexture` / `DataTexture`), explicitly set `texture.colorSpace =\n        THREE.SRGBColorSpace`. Leave data maps (normal, roughness, metalness) in\n        linear space (`NoColorSpace`).\n    -   **Grazing-Angle Sharpness**: Set `texture.anisotropy =\n        renderer.capabilities.getMaxAnisotropy();` on all procedural dials,\n        grids, and fine markings so they stay razor-sharp when viewed at shallow\n        camera angles.\n    -   **Tangents for Normal Maps**: When applying normal maps or anisotropy to\n        custom procedural geometries (`ExtrudeGeometry`, `LatheGeometry`), call\n        `geometry.computeTangents()`.\n\n5.  **Multi-Layer Glass, Decals & Transparency Sorting**:\n\n    -   **Glass Vessels & Domes**: Set `depthWrite: false` and explicit\n        `renderOrder` on outer glass layers so interior mechanisms remain crisp\n        and never flicker.\n    -   **Coplanar Z-Fighting**: When placing floor grids, chapter rings, or\n        decals directly on another surface, set `material.polygonOffset = true;\n        material.polygonOffsetFactor = -1; material.polygonOffsetUnits = -1;`.\n\n### 3. Studio Lighting, Area Softboxes & Contact Grounding\n\n1.  **Balanced Studio Rig (\342\211\244 3 Shadow Casters)**:\n    -   Configure a 3-point lighting rig (Key Light with `castShadow = true`,\n        tight orthographic shadow bounds `left/right/top/bottom = \302\261d`,\n        `shadow.bias = -0.0005`, `shadow.normalBias = 0.02`; Fill Light;\n        Rim/Backlight).\n    -   **Warm/Cool Color-Temperature Contrast & Horizon Fog**: Pair a warm\n        directional key light against a cool sky/ground `THREE.HemisphereLight`\n        fill (never rely on flat white `AmbientLight` alone), and match\n        `scene.fog` (`Fog` or `FogExp2`) to the horizon/sky background color so\n        distant geometry blends seamlessly.\n    -   **Studio Softboxes (`RectAreaLight`)**: For luxury product showcases\n        (watches, cars, jewelry), initialize `RectAreaLightUniformsLib.init()`\n        and add `THREE.RectAreaLight` studio light panels for authentic soft,\n        elongated specular highlights across metallic and glass bevels.\n2.  **Contact Shadow Grounding**:\n    -   Prevent hero objects from looking like they are floating in empty space\n        by placing a soft radial contact shadow plane directly beneath the\n        object (`transparent: true, depthWrite: false, opacity: 0.45`) or using\n        `ContactShadows`, and offset stacked horizontal surfaces (floors, roads,\n        rugs, decals) by `>= 0.02` on Y so coplanar faces never Z-fight.\n\n### 4. Runtime Performance, Kinematics & Raycasting Discipline\n\n1.  **Stable WebGL Lifecycle in React (`useRef` Container)**:\n    -   When mounting inside React, place the WebGL `<canvas>` inside a\n        dedicated empty `<div ref={canvasMountRef} />` outside sibling React HUD\n        overlays. Initialize `WebGLRenderer` and the scene once (`useEffect(...,\n        [])` with `cancelAnimationFrame` and `renderer.dispose()` on unmount),\n        call `renderer.compile(scene, camera)` once after setup to pre-warm\n        shaders, and store callbacks/mutable state in `useRef` so React UI state\n        updates never tear down the canvas or reset camera/actor positions.\n    -   Never apply CSS `backdrop-filter` / `backdrop-blur` on UI/HUD elements\n        over an active WebGL canvas (use solid or `rgba(...)` surfaces so\n        software WebGL rendering never stalls).\n2.  **Silky-Smooth Exponential Damping & Spring Kinematics**:\n    -   Never snap camera presets, exploded-view layer separations, or UI\n        transitions abruptly. Use frame-rate independent exponential damping\n        (`THREE.MathUtils.damp(current, target, lambda, delta)` or `1 -\n        Math.exp(-k * dt)`) or second-order spring physics (`stiffness`,\n        `damping`, `velocity`) inside the animation loop so movements glide with\n        physical weight and momentum.\n3.  **Zero Garbage Collection (GC) in the Render Loop**:\n    -   Pre-allocate reusable math helpers (`const _vec3 = new THREE.Vector3()`,\n        `_mat4`, `_quat`, `_color`) at module scope outside the animation loop.\n        Never instantiate `new THREE.Vector3()` inside the per-frame tick.\n4.  **Instancing & Frustum Culling Safety**:\n    -   Whenever rendering > 20 identical repeated items (screws, gear teeth,\n        stars, trees, grid nodes), group them into a `THREE.InstancedMesh`.\n        Always call `instancedMesh.computeBoundingSphere()` after setting\n        instance matrices (or set `frustumCulled = false` if instances move\n        dynamically) so instances never vanish when rotating the camera.\n5.  **High-Performance Raycasting**:\n    -   When implementing interactive hover/click inspection on complex models,\n        restrict raycasting to a dedicated layer (`raycaster.layers.set(1)`) or\n        invisible simplified proxy collision boxes, and throttle `pointermove`\n        raycasts so pointer movement never causes frame drops.\n\n### 5. Ergonomic UI Layout: Collapsible Bottom Dock (Zero Viewport Occlusion)\n\nWhen building your interactive UI overlay (`ControlOverlay.tsx` / HTML HUD),\nkeep the central 3D viewport 100% unobstructed so the user can admire your 3D\nwork without UI panels covering the model:\n\n```\n\342\224\214\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\220\n\342\224\202  [ Minimal Title / Spec Badge (Top-Left or Top-Center) ]   \342\224\202\n\342\224\202                                                            \342\224\202\n\342\224\202                                                            \342\224\202\n\342\224\202                     3D SCENE VIEWPORT                      \342\224\202\n\342\224\202                (100% Unobstructed Center)                  \342\224\202\n\342\224\202                                                            \342\224\202\n\342\224\202                                                            \342\224\202\n\342\224\202               [ \342\226\276 Collapse / Expand HUD ]                  \342\224\202\n\342\224\202  \342\224\214\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\220  \342\224\202\n\342\224\202  \342\224\202 \342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\227\217\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220 Exploded Slider | Finishes | Camera | Audio  \342\224\202  \342\224\202\n\342\224\202  \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\230  \342\224\202\n\342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\230\n```\n\n1.  **Unified Bottom Control Dock**:\n    -   Consolidate **all** interactive controls (exploded-view slider, material\n        finish swatches, lighting presets, camera view pills, time/speed\n        multipliers) into **one sleek, tabbed or compact horizontal glassmorphic\n        dock pinned to the bottom center** (`bottom: 20px; left: 50%; transform:\n        translateX(-50%); max-width: min(940px, 94vw);`).\n    -   **Never place floating control cards on the mid-left, mid-right, or\n        top-right flanks** where they collide with and cover the 3D subject.\n2.  **Collapsible \"Cinema / Zen Mode\" Drawer**:\n    -   Include a clean collapse/expand toggle button (`\342\226\276 Hide Controls` / `\342\226\264\n        Show Controls` or keyboard shortcut `H`) attached to the top edge of the\n        bottom dock.\n    -   When collapsed, smoothly slide the dock downward off-screen so the user\n        can inspect the 3D scene in full-screen glory or capture clean\n        screenshots.\n3.  **Clean, Static Status Indicators (No Pulsating Dots)**:\n    -   **Never use animated pulsating or glowing dots (`@keyframes pulse`)** in\n        status bars, badges, or buttons. Use clean, static typography, icons, or\n        subtle color swatches so the UI never distracts the eye from the 3D\n        motion.\n\n### 6. Quick Self-Audit Before Finalizing Code\n\n-   [ ] **Composite 3\342\200\22312 Part Procedural Models**: Are hero subjects, vehicles,\n    trees, and structures built as 3\342\200\22312 part `THREE.Group` meshes (with varied\n    roughness/metalness and grounded bottom-center pivots) rather than single\n    default primitives?\n-   [ ] **Environment Map & Warm/Cool Lighting**: Is `scene.environment`\n    populated via `RoomEnvironment` + `PMREMGenerator`, paired with a warm key\n    light, cool `HemisphereLight` fill, and horizon-matched `Fog` / `FogExp2`?\n-   [ ] **Texture Sharpness & Color Space**: Are procedural color textures set\n    to `SRGBColorSpace` with `anisotropy =\n    renderer.capabilities.getMaxAnisotropy()`?\n-   [ ] **Stable Canvas & No Side-Panel Clutter**: Is the WebGL `<canvas>`\n    mounted in an isolated ref container, with all interactive controls\n    consolidated into a **collapsible bottom dock** (no `backdrop-filter` over\n    WebGL and zero left/right side cards covering the 3D object)?\n-   [ ] **No Pulsating Dots**: Are all UI indicators static and free of\n    distracting `@keyframes pulse` dots?\n-   [ ] **Smooth Damping**: Are exploded-view separations and camera preset\n    transitions smoothly interpolated using `THREE.MathUtils.damp(..., delta)`\n    or spring physics?\n-   [ ] **Zero 404s**: Are all textures, materials, gear/component geometries,\n    and Web Audio sound effects 100% procedural?\n"
    num_lines: 335
    num_bytes: 18856
